# Linux (LF) newlines, in every text file, on every machine.
#
# Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true in its SYSTEM config. That leaves
# the committed blobs LF but hands the WORKING COPY CRLF, so a checkout there
# reads byte-different from the same commit on Linux or macOS while
# `git status` still reports clean.
#
# Declared here, the rule travels with the repository and no longer depends on
# how a given machine happens to be configured. Every text blob is ALREADY
# stored LF, verified byte-exactly: `git add --renormalize .` under this rule
# produces no index change, so nothing below rewrites history.

* text=auto eol=lf

# The test fixtures are real binaries -- numpy archives and one pickle -- and
# must never be touched. `text=auto` already detects them by their NUL bytes;
# naming them makes it certain rather than inferred, which is the difference
# between a rule that is correct and a rule that happens to work.

*.npz binary
*.pkl binary
